  #line-at(114.74pt, 331.64pt, ". Despite these broad opportunities, practical generation of adaptable, high-resolution ")
  #line-at(72pt, 359.24pt, "ultrasound fields remains technically challenging. ")
  #line-at(108.02pt, 386.84pt, "Existing ultrasound modulation systems can be broadly classified into two categories: ")
  #line-at(72pt, 414.46pt, "single-element transducer equipped with an additional lens, and multi‑element transducer array. ")
  #line-at(72pt, 442pt, "Lens-based systems shape wavefronts through tailored geometries or material properties, but most ")
  #line-at(72pt, 469.6pt, "lenses are fixed after fabrication and offer little or no reconfigurability. Mechanical scanning can ")
  #line-at(72pt, 497.2pt, "partially compensate for this inflexibility, yet inevitably leads to bulky platforms with slow refresh ")
  #line-at(72pt, 524.8pt, "rates and limited accessibility in confined or wearable settings. By contrast, multi‑element arrays ")
  #line-at(72pt, 552.4pt, "enable dynamic modulation through independent control of element amplitudes and delays. ")
  #line-at(72pt, 580pt, "However, ultrasound field synthesis typically requires hundreds to thousands of independently ")
  #line-at(72pt, 607.6pt, "driven channels, causing rapid escalation in hardware complexity, cost and power consumption. ")
  #line-at(72pt, 635.2pt, "Moreover, such systems are often bulky and mechanically rigid, making them difficult to conform ")
  #line-at(72pt, 662.8pt, "to curved geometries, including most biological surfaces and many non-biological objects. Recent ")
  #line-at(72pt, 690.42pt, "advances in flexible electronics have enabled soft, wearable ultrasound arrays")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 7.98pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(456.34pt, 685.9pt, "26,27")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(474.34pt, 690.42pt, " that mitigate ")
]

